 "sheet": "chapel_uk_01",
 "about": "A meeting house's or plain chapel's fittings, first for the Quaker Meeting House, Alton (list entry 1338904, Grade II*).",
 "evidence": [
  "List text: 'The gallery, erected in 1690, is of solid oak construction. Stop-chamfered posts support the gallery, and there are sturdy turned balusters to the balustrade. A folding screen is fitted beneath the gallery, with two hinged five-panel sections to either side of double three-panel doors ... The screen is painted on the lobby side, and unpainted within the meeting hall.'",
  "List text: 'At the north end of the meeting room is the bench or stand for the ministers and elders - the bench is raised on a dais, with tongue and groove dado panelling behind, and a screen to the front ... The hall has two ceiling beams, supported on chamfered timber pilasters.'",
  "List text: 'the original cottage is entered through a doorway protected by a hood on replacement carved brackets ... The 1832 cottage has a door opening with a matching hood.'",
  "Photos looked at, never copied (Commons, CC0 and CC BY-SA): the white rendered range under its long clay-tile roof, the big six-over-six sash, the brick and tile-hung wing on Church Street.",
  "Photos: two small gabled dormers over the cottages; 'Quaker Meeting House' painted in dark letters on the render beside the big sash."
 ],
